# Sprint 28a (1b, M13) — the aborts Windows is ALLOWED to have, by REASON.
#
# Each line is a substring; every abort the suite reports must match one.
# An abort matching nothing FAILS the job.
#
# PROVENANCE: these patterns are DERIVED FROM THE ASSUMPTION MESSAGES IN THE
# SOURCE, not from a Windows run of this branch — no Windows runner was
# available while 1b was written. The next matrix run is what confirms the set
# is complete; until then, treat an unbudgeted abort here as information rather
# than as a defect, and add it WITH its reason.

# The embedding corpus is a large artifact not carried in the repository.
no corpus at -Djawata.embed.corpus

# The profiler ranks SAMPLED measurements; under contention it can return too
# few samples to rank anything, and refuses to assert on a coin toss.
CPU profile too thin

# The RCP fixture stands in for a NATIVE eclipse launcher. A .cmd cannot exec
# the way the POSIX fake does, so the held JVM sits behind a cmd.exe the attach
# wait never times out on — the first attempt burned 75 runner-minutes before
# CI killed it (run 31789882384). An honest skip beats that.
the RCP launcher fake needs exec semantics a Windows .cmd cannot express

# A .cmd stand-in does not receive argv intact under ProcessBuilder's cmd.exe
# wrapping: the batch twin scans its whole argv for the -Dmdep.outputFile
# prefix and exits 3 on a miss, which run 31804158929 shows exactly. The
# sibling tests needing NO argv pass on Windows, so the invocation path itself
# is proven there. The java-launcher fake that removes this skip is M11.
the .cmd fake does not receive argv under cmd.exe wrapping
